needs:
title: Put fields before the body and drop desc:
desc:37
desc: is not a necessary cost. It exists to serve one layout choice —
fields after the body — and that choice is what creates the derived data.

Invert it. Header lines, then fields, then a blank line, then the body to
EOF:

  claim:
  needs: 3 8
  title: Write the pidly shell script
  effort: L
  handoff: Parser works; lexer fails on nested quotes.

  Body from here to the end of the file.

The body now ends at EOF, which nothing can spoof. Fields end at the first
blank line after line 3, which is positionally anchored before the body
starts, so body content cannot reach it — the objection that killed a
delimiter earlier does not apply. Verified against a body containing blank
lines and body lines that read as fields, plus empty-body and empty-field
tickets.

Every bounded read survives. Lines 1-2 still answer "can I take this",
line 3 is still the listing. Reading fields gets cheaper: a short scan from
the top rather than tail -n +N. Reading the body is a scan to the first
blank instead of a seek to a stored number — the same one lookup either way.

The payoff is that appending to a body needs no bookkeeping, which is the
operation the retraction in 19 made central, and principle 5 loses its only
exception.

Cost: every existing ticket must be rewritten, plus new, edit, get_field,
set_field, the tests and both documents. Mechanical, and there are only
about twenty tickets.
